# How this site works — writing principles

When creating or editing cards under **How this site works** (`howThisSiteWorksCards` / markdown in [`src/data/content.ts`](src/data/content.ts)), write for a **curious first-time visitor**, not for someone who already memorized the UI or the codebase.

Also keep the public card **Writing these explanations** (`howThisSiteWorksWritingMarkdown`) aligned if you change these principles.

## Core rule: show, then name

1. **Describe what someone can see or do on the page** (where it sits, what it looks like, what label is on the control).
2. **Then** introduce a short nickname if you need one (“trailer panel,” “frosted box”).
3. Never open with an unexplained nickname, file metaphor, or button text as if it were English vocabulary.

### Bad (assumes the reader shares your slang)

- “Imagine the glass card has a picture-frame opening…”
- “Learn more that lands in the right place…”
- “Normally a poster and a fullscreen embed would be enough…”

### Good (grounds first)

- “On the right side of the hero is a frosted, see-through panel with a still photo and a play button. That’s the trailer panel this article is about.”
- “Under that text is a button labeled **“Learn more.”** That label is just the current wording—the button is a call to action that opens different parts of the site depending on the moment in the video.”
- “Most sites show a still placeholder image, then play one video fullscreen. This panel does more than that.”

## UI labels are quotes, not jargon

- Button, menu, and prompt text (**“Learn more”**, **“Please choose a viewing mode”**, **“Go back”**, **Interactive**) must be **quoted** and identified as on-screen labels.
- Explain the **job** of the control (call to action, mode choice, exit) in plain words. Do not weave the bare label into a run-on sentence as if it were a technical term.

## Industry slang is optional, never required

- Words like “poster,” “chrome,” “portal,” “cue rail,” “glass card” are **not** assumed knowledge.
- Prefer everyday language (placeholder image, browser clutter, floating layer, chapter text under the video).
- If you mention slang, define it in one clause, then prefer the plain term afterward.

## Audience and tone

- Assume the reader is **new to this site** and **not an engineer**.
- Slow down. Prefer short paragraphs and concrete metaphors (picture frame, door into the rest of the site, DVD chapters) over compressed systems-speak.
- File paths and API names belong in a short “for people who want the file names” section—not in the opening pitch.
- Modal **title / intro / excerpt** must follow the same rules as the markdown body.

## Scope

Applies to new How-this-site-works articles and substantial rewrites of existing ones. Older port/theme logs may keep historical voice; when you touch them heavily, raise clarity toward these principles.
